Stress tests: slow, optional, and run under valgrind, so they are not part
of the regular suite in ../testsuite.  They work the same way: a <test>.sh
and <test>.good pair, ./runtest.sh <test> to run one and diff its output
against the .good, ./runall.sh for all of them.  Build the library and the
programs in .. first (make, or the tests target of the cmake build).

Each .sh builds its fixture modules under tmp/<test>/ from the import files
in ../testsuite, runs a program from .. in a loop under valgrind with full
leak checking, and prints what the program answered followed by valgrind's
leak and error summary with the process id stripped.  So a test fails on a
wrong answer, on a definite leak, or on any memory error (--error-exitcode
makes the exit code part of the output).

flatapi	the flat C API (bindings/flatapi.cpp, what the Swift and other
	non-C++ bindings call): every entry point short of InstallMgr and
	BibleSync, handles made, used and freed over and over, by
	../flatapitest.  A count argument to flatapitest sets how often;
	the output does not depend on it.

To add a test, write it as in ../testsuite and make its .good with:

	./mytest.sh > mytest.good

after checking the output by hand.
